Artifact 1 — Main Paper
Scale-Transition Annotation Graphs (STAG): A Methodological Framework and Scoping-Review Protocol for Annotating Scale-Dependent Organization
Abstract
Scientific descriptions of complex systems are distributed across scales, but no shared annotation standard records how those descriptions relate while preserving distinctions between derivation, representation, evidence, counterfactual dependence, and interpretation. We propose Scale-Transition Annotation Graphs (STAG), a specification and pre-registration rather than a validated framework. Nodes are tuples of system scope, model, and granularity; domain and regime are partition-relative annotation attributes; composite formation is represented by event nodes mapping to PROV-O prov:Activity. The framework separates a core tier of five rated fields from an extension tier containing optional fields, a scale-separation record, and seven derived display labels. The flagship label STAG-Discovered records a model-relative intervention result under four prerequisites that are necessary and sufficient for the label. A separate field, evidential_anchoring, records whether the annotation's evidence satisfies four further conditions and is reported alongside the label. The framework reports the anchoring status of every STAG-Discovered annotation and makes no claim that any annotation is currently anchored. We report one illustrative worked example that is a STAG-Discovered annotation and is not anchored, and we state why it is not anchored. The scoped search that would test the contribution claim has not been executed. No inter-rater reliability study has been performed. The companion codebook and schema are deposited as a separate artifact; the response to hostile readings is deposited as a second separate artifact.

Keywords: effective field theory, renormalization group, multiscale modelling, annotation, provenance, counterfactual dependence, inter-rater reliability

1. Introduction
Scientific knowledge is organized by scale. A single physical system may be described using microscopic fields, composite particles, collective variables, or macroscopic structures. These descriptions are related by inter-theoretic transformations—coarse-graining, integration-out procedures, symmetry-breaking, and renormalization-group flows—but the relations themselves are rarely made explicit in a reusable, machine-readable form. This raises a foundational question in the philosophy of science: how should inter-scale relations be represented in a way that preserves distinctions between derivation, representation, evidence, counterfactual dependence, and interpretation, without collapsing differences in mechanism or evidential status?

This question sits at the intersection of several philosophical and scientific traditions. The philosophy of inter-theoretic relations has long examined reduction, emergence, and asymptotic reasoning (Batterman 2002; Butterfield 2011; Wimsatt 2007; Dizadji-Bahmani et al. 2010). Theories of scientific representation have analyzed how models relate to target systems (Giere 1988; Frigg 2006; Suppes 1967). Formal ontology and knowledge representation have developed tools for entities, relations, and provenance (Arp et al. 2015; Smith et al. 2007). Mature physical traditions—effective field theory, renormalization-group methods, multiscale modelling—address parts of the problem within specific domains (Weinberg 1979; Wilson 1971; E 2011). Yet none of these traditions supplies a general representational infrastructure for recording how scale-dependent descriptions relate while preserving the distinctions listed above.

STAG (Scale-Transition Annotation Graphs) proposes such an infrastructure. It is an annotation layer above domain-specific theories. The central claim is that claims about transitions between scale-dependent descriptions can be recorded, compared, and re-used across scientific domains in a common, provenance-aware structural language, without collapsing differences in mechanism, evidential status, and interpretive context. STAG answers by representing selected effective organizations as nodes and annotated relationships as transitions in a directed multigraph. Established theories remain responsible for physical derivation; STAG records how a transition claim is represented, classified, and evidenced.

Three philosophical commitments define the scope. First, STAG does not identify levels of nature; it annotates candidate descriptive units and tests whether the annotations are reproducible. This is a methodological agnosticism about ontological strata: the framework records representational structure without committing to metaphysical levels. Second, STAG separates the annotation of a transition claim from any statistical inference about gaps in empirical distributions. Third, STAG is a specification whose usefulness is an empirical question. It is offered as a formal methodology in the tradition of Suppes (1967): a precise, testable framework whose adequacy is to be assessed by application.

The framework was previously referred to informally as "Assembly Graphs." That name conflicts with established terminology in genomics and sequence bioinformatics. The framework is now named Scale-Transition Annotation Graphs (STAG); the word "assembly" is retained only as a descriptive term for cases involving composite formation. The framework does not separately define "scale transition." The four prerequisites in Section 7.5 are the sole operational criteria for STAG-Discovered.

1.1 Scope and nature of the contribution
The paper's contribution is a structured annotation language for recording scale-transition claims in a way that preserves distinctions between derivation, representation, evidence, counterfactual dependence, and interpretation. The language is designed to be internally consistent, with fields stated with sufficient precision to be applied by a second annotator, and with evidential distinctions reported alongside the annotation rather than folded into it.

The paper is offered as a specification and pre-registration protocol, not as a validated empirical instrument. Its contribution is representational and methodological rather than empirical. The framework's internal distinctions are proposed as useful analytic categories for annotation, not as claims about natural kinds. The validation programme is a plan for future empirical work. The flagship label (STAG-Discovered) is defined with operational criteria, but the paper does not assert that any annotation currently satisfies the stronger evidential status defined in Section 1.2.

The framework's predicates are stated as operational definitions for annotation purposes, not as formally well-founded metaphysical categories. The entailment basis is a classification scheme for annotation, not a decision procedure for logical entailment. The graph formalism is a labelled directed multigraph, chosen for its simplicity and compatibility with existing provenance standards. The framework's conventions are chosen for their utility in annotation.

1.2 The two statuses: STAG-Discovered and evidential anchoring
The framework separates two statuses for a transition annotation. They are reported side by side and they carry different content.

STAG-Discovered is assigned to an annotation that satisfies the four prerequisites of Section 7.5. It records that, within a declared model and partition, a specified intervention on a specified contribution materially changes a specified outcome, and that a scale-separation record is present with a ratio at or above the declared minimum. The status is a substantive classification of the annotation. It is not a claim about the quality of the evidence behind the annotation.

Evidential anchoring is a distinct and stronger status. The word "anchored" is used in preference to "supported" because "supported" has a statistical meaning in the validation programme. A STAG-Discovered annotation is evidentially anchored if and only if all four of the conditions below hold.

Condition (a), prerequisite evidence. Each of the four prerequisites of Section 7.5 is satisfied, and the evidence supporting each prerequisite is appropriate for that prerequisite. For the effective-description-change prerequisite, the evidence is formal application of one or more of the criteria in Section 4.1 to the model. For the non-definitional prerequisite, the evidence is the absence of a citable entailment result settling the outcome, with the search for such a result recorded. For the material-change prerequisite, the evidence is a computation performed by the annotator, or a specific citation to a study of the annotated model at the annotated parameters, with citation_scope = annotated_model. For the scale-separation prerequisite, the evidence is a scale-separation record satisfying the requirement stated in Section 7.5.

Condition (b), control-parameter robustness. The scale-separation ratio exceeds the declared minimum throughout the pre-registered control-parameter band, and the band is externally anchored. The band is externally anchored if it is read from an external source. The band is recorded as band_source = external_reference with band_source_citation populated. If band_source = analyst_chosen, condition (b) is not satisfied.

Condition (c), evidence independence. The material-change evidence is independent of the assumptions used to fix node identity, the regime attribute, and the effective-description-change classification. The annotation records independence_of_material_change_evidence with value independent, shared_assumption, or not_assessed. Evidential anchoring requires independent.

Condition (d), test-result independence. The material-change result is not entailed by applying the material-change test's classification scheme to the assumptions used to compute the result. The annotation records material_change_test_result_independence with value independent, shared_assumption, or not_assessed. Evidential anchoring requires independent.

Prerequisite satisfaction is a condition on the annotation. Evidential anchoring is a condition on the evidence supporting the annotation's satisfaction of the prerequisites. A STAG-Discovered annotation can fail to be evidentially anchored. The annotation records the anchoring status in the field evidential_anchoring_status. The display layer reports the same value under the field evidential_anchoring. The framework does not claim that any annotation is currently anchored.

The four anchoring conditions are not of the same logical type. Condition (a) is a condition on the annotation record. Condition (b) is a condition on the annotated system and the band. Condition (c) is a condition on the relation between the material-change evidence and the assumptions that fix other fields. Condition (d) is a condition on the relation between the material-change test and its result. The framework reports the four conditions individually and does not combine them into a single score.

Three target profiles are stated for the anchoring status, in decreasing order of evidential strength.

Profile (i), measured-measured. A system with an externally measured microscopic scale and an externally measured collective scale, in a regime where the ratio exceeds the minimum across an externally anchored band.

Profile (ii), measured-inferred. A system with an externally measured microscopic scale and a collective scale inferred from external data via a model that does not take the annotated model as input. The inference source is recorded in the sub-field scale_inference_source.

Profile (iii), synthetic. A synthetic construction with two independently specified scales, where neither scale is a function of the annotated model's control parameters, and where a robustness band is declared.

1.3 Display labels
The display labels are STAG-Discovered, STAG-Definitional, STAG-None, STAG-Intra, STAG-Intra-Definitional, STAG-Intra-Non_definitional, and STAG-Unclassified. In space-limited tables they may be abbreviated STAG-DISC, STAG-DEF, STAG-NONE, STAG-INTRA, STAG-INTRA-DEF, STAG-INTRA-ND, and STAG-UNC.

STAG-Intra is the label used when no intervention has been attempted on an intra-domain transition, that is, when intervention_classification = Not_applicable. The other two intra-domain labels are STAG-Intra-Definitional and STAG-Intra-Non_definitional.

The display record always contains the derived field evidential_anchoring. Its permitted values are anchored, anchored_with_stated_limitations, candidate, preliminary, not_assessed, and not_applicable. For STAG-Discovered annotations, the value is a copy of evidential_anchoring_status. For all other labels, the value is not_applicable. A STAG-Discovered display label is never reported without the anchoring marker.

The field evidential_anchoring_status is the annotation-record name and evidential_anchoring is the display-layer name. The two names carry the same permitted values and the same content. The annotation record uses the longer name; the display record uses the shorter name.

1.4 Status, intended venue, and companion artifacts
This is a methodological and scoping-review protocol proposal. No empirical validation has been performed. Because the paper presents a specification and a pre-registration, its appropriate venue class is a registered-report, protocol, or philosophy-of-science outlet, for example Synthese, The British Journal for the Philosophy of Science, European Journal for Philosophy of Science, or Erkenntnis. The paper is not directed at a methods journal that expects empirical results.

Two companion artifacts accompany the main paper. The codebook and schema (Artifact 2) contains the operational instrument, the field list, the permitted value sets, and the semantic validation rules. The response to hostile readings (Artifact 3) contains the framework's replies to objections that a hostile referee may raise and that are not part of the specification itself. The main paper defers to the codebook and schema for field-by-field specifications and to the response for extended discussion of objections.

The companion statistical protocol is a separate document in preparation. Its separation from the annotation framework is a deliberate methodological choice: the framework records a transition claim and its evidential status; the statistical protocol tests for empirical gaps.

1.5 Proposed contribution and scoping-review decision rules
The proposed contribution is the integration of effective field theory, renormalization-group methods, multiscale modelling, ontology, and formal causal modelling into a single versionable, provenance-aware annotation architecture. This is a conditional contribution, asserted subject to confirmation by the scoped search.

The scoped literature search is described in Supplementary Material D. Its protocol, including search terms, databases, inclusion criteria, a PRISMA-like flow, and a coding manual for the four features below, is drafted and will be deposited with the pre-registration. The comparison against PROV-O, Nanopublications, MIRIAM, SBML, SBGN, BioPAX, EMMO, CMIP/ES-DOC, BFO, and relevant multiscale modelling frameworks is a draft whose entries will be confirmed against the executed search. The search has not been executed.

The four features are operationalized as follows. Feature (a), scale-dependent effective-description criteria: present if and only if the candidate system specifies a machine-readable or formally stated criterion for when a description at one scale is replaced by a description at another, not merely a nesting or part-of relation. Feature (b), threshold provenance by parameter source: present if and only if the candidate system specifies, for each threshold or scale boundary, a recorded origin from among theoretical, empirical, and conventional, with a source field and a rule for combining origins. Feature (c), model-relative counterfactual dependence: present if and only if the candidate system specifies an intervention on a declared model plus a material-change test, evaluated relative to a declared domain partition. Feature (d), multi-label status tracking: present if and only if the candidate system specifies independent, simultaneously recordable statuses for physical claim, graph mapping, and implementation, with revision provenance. Each feature is coded present, partial, or absent, with a justification field and a second-coder check.

Three outcomes are pre-registered. Outcome (i). If the search finds no system combining features (a) through (d), the conditional contribution is supported, subject to the search's scope limitations. Outcome (ii). If the search finds a system with one or more but not all features, the conditional contribution narrows to the integration of the missing features. Outcome (iii). If the search finds a system combining all four features, the conditional contribution is retracted and the paper is restructured as a comparison and extension proposal.

1.6 Core tier, extension tier, and design rationale
The core tier contains the minimum annotation required to record a transition claim reproducibly. The five core-tier fields are the regime attribute of Section 5.2, the node-identity decision of Section 5.1, the effective-description change of Section 4.2, the intervention classification of Section 7.1, and the cross-domain counterfactual dependence of Section 7.2. Of these five, regime and effective-description change are partly externally anchored. Node identity is externally anchored only for the M component. The intervention classification is expertise-dependent and is validated with expertise-stratified agreement. Cross-domain counterfactual dependence has no independent expert reference under the annotation protocol itself.

The extension tier contains optional fields and the scale-separation record: effective-description change kind, threshold provenance, characteristic scale, reversibility, multi-label status, partition sensitivity, scale-separation record, intervention kind, uncertainty and alternatives, and event-node metadata.

Display labels are derived from the core-tier fields and the extension-tier scale-separation record. The evidential anchoring status is a separate extension-tier field computed from the four anchoring conditions and is reported alongside the label. Both are validated as a unit in Phase 2, after the core-tier fields have been validated individually.

The framework is deliberately elaborate because a claim about a scale transition should carry enough structure to be re-usable by another analyst without access to the original author's mind. Elaboration carries a cost: rules may be too complex to apply reliably, or may encode distinctions that human annotators do not in fact use. The core/extension split and the validation programme are the mechanisms through which this risk is tested.

2. Scope and Non-Claims
Methodological non-claims. STAG does not propose a theory of everything, a replacement for any established physical theory, a new fundamental interaction, a theory of quantum gravity, a parameter-free theory of emergence, predictions of new particles without a generative theory, a complete graph of nature, or a universal assembly mechanism. It does not claim that graph similarity establishes physical similarity, that removal of a domain contribution in a model establishes a general causal property of the physical transition, or that the framework can determine whether a proposed transition is physically real. It does not claim that its predicates are formally well-founded or that its graph formalism is more than a labelled directed multigraph.

Physical non-claims. STAG does not claim that every effective description corresponds to a new fundamental degree of freedom, that any domain partition is universally fundamental, that all scale transitions are sharp, or that empirical gaps necessarily correspond to physical transitions. STAG annotates transition claims on top of established phase structures. Where no phase diagram exists, STAG records the not-determinable state for regime-dependent fields.

Label non-claims. STAG does not claim that a display label is an intrinsic property of a transition, or that every transition must receive one. It does not claim that a STAG-Discovered classification establishes a universal causal fact. A STAG-Discovered label records that, within a declared model and domain partition, removing a specified contribution materially changes a specified transition under a specified non-definitional intervention, with the four prerequisites of Section 7.5 satisfied. Evidential anchoring is a separate status, reported alongside the label. The framework does not claim that its constructs have been validated for inter-rater reliability, that its elaborateness is empirically justified, or that the display labels correspond to distinct natural kinds.

The narrower claim. STAG supplies a structured, provenance-aware annotation language for recording claims about scale-dependent effective organization, together with procedures for defining descriptive units, recording threshold provenance, recording model-relative cross-domain counterfactual dependence, representing evidential status, and maintaining revision history.

3. Related Work
3.1 Effective field theory and the renormalization group. Effective field theory and renormalization-group methods supply rigorous criteria for many scale-dependent changes (Weinberg 1979; Wilson 1971; Lepage 1989; Burgess 2007). Where those criteria directly answer the scientific question, they take precedence. STAG is not a renormalization-group flow.

3.2 Multiscale modelling. Multiscale methods couple models at different resolutions (E 2011; E et al. 2007). STAG records that descriptions are related without requiring a particular numerical coupling algorithm.

3.3 Emergence, hierarchy, and levels of organization. Philosophical and scientific literature addresses reduction, asymptotic reasoning, autonomy, and explanatory structure (Batterman 2002; Butterfield 2011; Simon 1962; Wimsatt 2007), including mechanistic levels (Bechtel 2008; Craver 2007; Potochnik 2017; Love 2012). STAG does not resolve metaphysical questions about levels.

3.4 Counterfactual and interventionist reasoning. Woodward's (2003) manipulationist account and Pearl's (2000) formal treatment of counterfactuals ground the claim that a contribution is counterfactually relevant to an outcome when an explicit intervention on that contribution changes the outcome under otherwise matched conditions. STAG does not claim to establish causal identification in the sense required by statistical or structural causal-inference frameworks. The framework's dependence field is model-relative and does not satisfy the identification conditions of the potential-outcomes or structural-equations literatures.

3.5 Ontology, provenance, and annotation standards. Adjacent work includes the Basic Formal Ontology (Arp, Smith, and Spear 2015) and the OBO Foundry (Smith et al. 2007); PROV-O (Lebo et al. 2013) and Nanopublications (Groth et al. 2010; Mons et al. 2011) for provenance; MIRIAM (Le Novère et al. 2005) for model annotation; and Bechhofer et al. (2013) on the gap between linked-data publication and scientific practice. Domain-specific standards include SBML, SBGN, BioPAX, MIRIAM, EMMO, and CMIP/ES-DOC. None specifies how a model at one scale relates to a model at another through a model-relative counterfactual transition rule. STAG's mapping to PROV-O is specified in Section 8.5.

3.6 Graph-based and multiscale annotation efforts. Prior work includes hierarchical process graphs and multilayer networks (Kivelä et al. 2014; Boccaletti et al. 2014). STAG differs in three ways. Its transitions carry a model-relative counterfactual-dependence classification rather than only a relationship type. Its node identity is governed by a tuple-based rule decoupled from transition classification and from the domain partition. It treats the domain partition as an analyst-declared and versioned input.

3.7 Statistical gap detection. Methods for identifying gaps and discontinuities are well established (Kulldorff 1997; Glaz et al. 2001). STAG performs none of this work. The companion statistical protocol handles inference.

3.8 Comparison with existing standards (draft). The comparison is a draft whose entries will be confirmed against the executed search. The coding manual for features (a) through (d) is stated in Section 1.5. The draft comparison and the entries for each standard are deposited in Supplementary Material D. The current draft indicates that STAG as proposed specifies all four features and that the seven standards named above each specify at most two of the four, with none specifying model-relative counterfactual dependence in the sense of feature (c). If the executed search confirms this pattern, outcome (i) of Section 1.5 is taken.

4. Operational Definitions
4.1 Effective description
A scale-appropriate representation using variables, collective quantities, approximations, or degrees of freedom appropriate to a specified regime. It qualifies if at least one criterion fires.

ΔDOF fires if the effective state space or algebra of observables changes, and the change is not an exact reparametrization. The operational test is to provide two observables whose algebra differs between the two descriptions, with a citation. Integrating out a field within the same effective theory, and other coarse-grainings retaining the same algebra, do not by themselves fire ΔDOF. The criterion is stated as a test rather than as a theorem, and the test's soundness rests on the annotator's ability to identify two observables whose algebra differs under the declared notion of algebra. The framework does not fix the notion of algebra, and this is a source of rater disagreement that the validation programme (Section 13) is designed to measure.

ΔPC fires if the power-counting or expansion parameter governing the regime changes. ΔSYM fires if the symmetry realization or symmetry-breaking pattern changes. ΔAPP fires if the controlled approximation scheme changes and the previous scheme has a stated breakdown or declared accuracy limit that the new scheme addresses. ΔEFT fires if a different effective field theory is required for the stated precision, externally justified by a stated breakdown of the previous EFT, an independently specified accuracy requirement, a change in relevant degrees of freedom, a change in validity regime, or an empirically demonstrated failure of the previous model. ΔCOL fires if new collective variables become appropriate and are not reducible to a change of basis within the existing description. ΔBIND fires if a new stable or metastable composite or bound-state organisation acquires its own effective description. EXPERT fires if domain experts independently identify distinct regimes where the formal criteria are unavailable.

ΔAPP and ΔEFT require external justification. EXPERT requires at least three independent experts, with independent classifications and reported inter-expert agreement. The solution method or approximation scheme is a node attribute, not part of node identity.

ΔDOF is the broadest criterion; its prevalence must be reported. Negative controls must be included. Pairs differing only by an exact reparametrization (basis change, field redefinition, Kramers–Wannier duality, gauge fixing, equivalent mean-field reorganization) should not satisfy any formal criterion. ΔDOF-only cases are reported separately. The framework does not prove that the negative-control set is exhaustive; the reported false-positive rate is a lower bound on the criterion's actual error rate over the space of exact reparametrizations.

4.2 Effective-description change
A transition qualifies as demonstrating an effective-description change only when at least one of the criteria ΔDOF through ΔBIND is positively demonstrated with recorded evidence, or EXPERT is used under the constraints above. A transition satisfying none is annotated "No demonstrated effective-description change."

Yes-strong is recorded if ΔDOF fires with at least one other formal criterion, or if a non-ΔDOF criterion fires. Yes-weak is recorded if ΔDOF fires alone. No is recorded if no criterion fires. Uncertain is recorded if the annotation cannot decide. Yes-weak transitions are excluded from STAG-Discovered and receive STAG-Unclassified with reason weak_effective_description_basis. The framework distinguishes three cases not assumed identical: a different physical state, a different descriptive representation, and a demonstrated effective-description change. Only the third is recorded as Yes.

4.3 Node
A pragmatic descriptive unit represented as a graph node. It does not imply an ontological stratum. Defined by a three-component tuple. The domain of the description is a separate, partition-relative annotation attribute.

5. Node Identity and Individuation
5.1 The node identity tuple
A node N is the tuple N = (S, M, G), where S is system scope, M is model or formalism, and G is annotation granularity declared before annotation begins.

The declared domain D is an annotation attribute, with domain_id, partition_id, and partition_version, not a component of node identity. A node can be assigned to different domains under different partitions without becoming a different node.

Because domain is not part of identity, two descriptions sharing S, M, and G are the same node even if assigned to different domains under different partitions or used to study different primary observables. The framework records domain assignment, primary observable, and domains spanned as attributes, and flags domain mismatch on the transition. This removes intervention-dependence of identity at the cost of potentially under-splitting descriptions a coarser analysis might keep distinct. The identity-splitting audit of Section 5.8 is the diagnostic.

The flag domain_mismatch is true if and only if intervention_target_domain is not a member of the set of domains to which the source and target nodes are assigned under the annotation's declared partition_id and partition_version. It does not change node identity; it is recorded on the transition and logged for audit. For STAG-Discovered, STAG-Definitional, and the STAG-Intra variants, the flag is a substantive limitation. For STAG-None and STAG-Unclassified, it is a diagnostic.

Regime is an annotation attribute, not an identity component. Two descriptions sharing S, M, and G are the same node even if they apply in different regimes; the difference is shown as a self-loop with a regime-change attribute.

Solution method, approximation scheme, scientific purpose, validity regime, primary observable, and domains spanned are attributes, not identity components.

The node-identity decision is a core-tier rated field with values merged, new_node, uncertain, and not_determinable.

5.2 Regime attribute
Every annotation records a regime attribute: symmetric (the relevant order parameter vanishes); broken (the order parameter is nonzero); crossover-band (the order parameter changes continuously without a sharp transition); not_applicable (no order parameter is defined); not_determinable (available information does not support an assignment). Where a domain uses specialized vocabulary, the annotation records both the domain-specific label and its framework mapping.

For symmetric, broken, or crossover-band, the annotation records the order parameter, the control-parameter point at which the order-parameter value was determined, and the source from which the point's location on the phase diagram was read. For crossovers, the value-to-label mapping is expressed as value bands declared before annotation, recorded in the field value_bands_if_crossover. The bands are recorded with regime_band_source, with permitted values external_reference and analyst_chosen, and with a citation when external_reference. If regime_band_source = analyst_chosen, the annotation records regime_band_analyst_chosen = true, and the display label is retained with the flag attached and displayed alongside.

The crossover value bands are distinct from the control-parameter band of condition (b) of evidential anchoring: the crossover value bands partition the order-parameter axis for the regime attribute, while the control-parameter band is the interval of the scale-separation record over which the ratio must exceed the minimum.

The placement of the control-parameter point is read from a previously established source. Where no such external placement is available, the regime attribute is not_determinable.

If either endpoint's regime is not_determinable, the annotation records regime_not_determinable = true. The display label is computed as usual and retained with the flag attached and displayed alongside. A STAG-Discovered case may carry the flag if the phase diagram is unavailable while the scale-separation record is available from independent sources.

5.3 Pre-registration of tuple-matching rules
Before annotation begins, the application must declare a matching rule for each component.

S matches if and only if the two descriptions have the same scope-nesting position, declared in the model reference. M matches if and only if the two descriptions have the same Hamiltonian or Lagrangian. G matches if and only if the declared granularity labels are equal.

The default M-equivalence transformations are exact reparametrizations: field redefinition, basis change, gauge fixing, and exact duality transformations. When an M-equivalence is claimed, the annotation records m_equivalence_basis (the transformation type) and m_equivalence_witness (the specific transformation written out). A merged decision on two descriptions with different Lagrangians and either field missing is a validation error.

Integrating out a field yields the same M only if the resulting action is the exact effective action at the declared scale, with no truncation, projection, or retention of a subset of channels.

A truncation is a declared-order truncation if and only if the expansion is explicitly stated in the model reference, the order is explicitly stated, and the truncation retains all terms up to and including that order. Anything else is a sectoral or channel truncation, produces a different M, and is recorded as a derived-from edge (Section 8.5).

Two descriptions merge into one node if and only if all three component rules report a match. Record the matching-rule version and the M-equivalence rule version. The application must record a justification for the choice of domain partition.

5.4 Worked identity examples
Example A, 2D Ising at two temperatures (merged; self-loop). D1 at T equal to twice Tc, D2 at T equal to 0.5 Tc. S matches (both apply to the 2D Ising ferromagnet); M matches (same Hamiltonian); G matches (both coarse). Same node. D1 symmetric, D2 broken. Self-loop with regime-change attribute.

Example B, Fermi liquid vs. BCS mean field (new_node). E1 = Fermi-liquid effective action; E2 = BCS reduced Hamiltonian (phonons integrated out of the Fröhlich Hamiltonian, only the pairing channel retained). S and G match; M does not, because retaining only the pairing channel is a channel truncation, not a declared-order truncation. Derived-from edge with derivation_kind = truncation. Decision: new_node.

Example C, gauge fixing (merged). SU(2) in Landau gauge vs. Feynman gauge. Gauge fixing is an exact reparametrization. m_equivalence_basis = gauge_fixing; m_equivalence_witness = the gauge condition. Decision: merged.

Example D, electroweak vs. Fermi theory (new_node). Fermi theory obtained from the electroweak theory by integrating out W and Z with a declared-order truncation to the dimension-six four-fermion operator. M does not match. Derived-from edge with derivation_kind = truncation, derivation_specification = "operator-dimension truncation at dimension six".

5.5 Purpose as attribute of use
Scientific purpose is recorded as an attribute of the annotation. A single node may serve multiple purposes.

5.6 Granularity and boundary cases
Granularity is declared before annotation. Nested descriptions are not automatically identical.

5.7 Identity sensitivity and validation
Node identity is a primary reliability target. Node-set agreement is reported before any transition is annotated, and is measured on pairs of candidate descriptions that differ in at least one tuple component. This is the primary definition of the plausible-merge set. A sensitivity analysis uses pairs that differ in exactly one tuple component.

Because regime and domain are attributes rather than identity components, two descriptions sharing S, M, and G are definitionally the same node, so node-set agreement is tested only on pairs differing in at least one of S, M, G. The externally anchored component of node identity is M; S and G are settled by the pre-registered matching rule and are not independently checked.

5.8 Identity-splitting audit
For every merged node used in a cross-domain claim (STAG-Discovered, STAG-Definitional, or any STAG-Intra variant), the annotation records splitting_partition_id (an alternative admissible partition under which the node would be split), splitting_would_change_label (a boolean recording whether the derived display label would change under that partition), and splitting_audit_note (the grounds on which the node would or would not split).

The audit does not apply to new_node decisions. For a new_node case, partition sensitivity under Section 7.8 is the required check instead.

Where splitting_would_change_label = true, the label is reported with identity_partition_sensitive = true attached. The case is logged for audit and reported in a separate partition-sensitive stratum in the validation results. No automatic exclusion or downgrade.

6. Threshold Provenance (Extension Tier)
Each threshold is decomposed into its materially relevant components. Each component receives one or more provenance labels: theoretical (specified by a governing theory); empirical (estimated from a dataset, with the dataset recorded and the overlap with the transition's own data noted); conventional (reporting or operational role). A summary label may be computed: Class 1 (theoretical or independently estimated empirical); Class 2 (at least one component empirically calibrated using the same dataset as the transition); Class 3 (conventional); Mixed; Unknown. A conventional threshold is not scientifically inferior; the label describes origin, not quality.

7. Transition Attributes and Derived Display Labels
7.1 Primary independent attributes (core tier)
The core-tier attributes are as follows.

effective_description_change takes values Yes-strong, Yes-weak, No, Uncertain.

cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence takes values Yes, No, Definitional_only, Not_tested, Intra_domain, Untestable, Uncertain, and not_determinable.

intervention_classification takes values Definitional, Definitional(expertise-dependent), Non_definitional, Not_applicable, and not_determinable. The underlying entailment structure is recorded in the sub-field entailment_basis, which takes values E1, E2, E3, E4, and not_determinable. The mapping is as follows. intervention_classification = Definitional if and only if entailment_basis is E1 or E2. intervention_classification = Definitional(expertise-dependent) if and only if entailment_basis is E4. intervention_classification = Non_definitional if and only if entailment_basis is E3. intervention_classification = Not_applicable if and only if no intervention is attempted. intervention_classification = not_determinable when the entailment basis cannot be determined from the available sources; the annotation is retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside.

The four entailment basis values are defined as follows. E1, structural entailment: the outcome follows from the symbolic form of the surviving equations alone, with no numerical or self-consistent solution required, and no applicability condition requires separate confirmation. E2, entailment with conditions confirmed: a specific citable result settles the outcome by entailment, and each applicability condition is confirmed by a recorded witness. E3, requires new computation: no cited result settles the outcome, and establishing it requires a new numerical or self-consistent solution. E4, contested: a cited entailment result exists but is contested, or its applicability conditions cannot be confirmed, or any applicability condition lacks a witness.

The entailment basis is a classification and not a decision procedure. The phrase "follows from the symbolic form" is used in the ordinary mathematical sense and is not given a proof-theoretic semantics. The classification is bounded by the E4 label. The not_determinable value is used when the sources required to apply the classification are unavailable or unverifiable; a not_determinable entailment basis propagates to intervention_classification = not_determinable and to cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = not_determinable.

For E1 and E2, the annotation records cited_structural_result with result_reference, applicability_conditions, and conditions_check as a list of entries each having condition and condition_witness. The condition_witness must name a specific feature of the intervention or the surviving description, identified by an equation, a term, a kernel, a parameter range, or a regime condition, and must cite the source establishing it. If any condition lacks a witness, entailment_basis must be E4. The field takes the value not_determinable when the source supporting the entailment cannot be verified.

For E3 with a numerical citation, the annotation records cited_numerical_result with citation, result_summary, classification_effect, and citation_scope (values annotated_model, related_model, other, and not_determinable). The field takes the value not_determinable when the cited numerical values cannot be verified against their sources.

intervention_source_domains and intervention_target_domain record the domains involved.

intervention_kind takes values boundary_term, defining_coupling, subdominant_coupling, other. If intervention_kind = defining_coupling, the annotation records defining_coupling_intervention = true, requires a second independent rater, and is excluded from STAG-Discovered unless the second rater independently confirms the intervention classification and the material-change result.

cross_domain_intervention_available takes values Yes, No, Uncertain.

partition_id and partition_version identify the partition.

domain_mismatch is a boolean defined in Section 5.1.

7.2 Cross-domain counterfactual dependence
Yes is recorded when a substantive intervention was performed, the intervention is non-definitional, and the outcome is a material change under a pre-specified material-change test. No is recorded when an intervention was performed, the intervention is non-definitional, and no material change is observed. Definitional_only is recorded when an intervention was performed, was classified Definitional or Definitional(expertise-dependent), and is cross-domain, and the outcome follows from a cited entailment result; this value is mandatory in that case and forbidden when the intervention is not cross-domain. Not_tested is recorded when no intervention was attempted; it is the counterpart of intervention_classification = Not_applicable. Intra_domain is recorded when an intervention was performed, all intervention_source_domains lie within a single declared domain, and the transition's observable is situated in that same domain. Untestable is recorded when no defensible intervention or decomposition is available under the declared model. Uncertain is recorded when the annotation cannot decide between the above. not_determinable is recorded when the field cannot be assigned because a prerequisite field it depends on is itself not_determinable.

Cross-domain is defined by the intervention: intervention_source_domains together with intervention_target_domain span at least two distinct declared domains.

7.3 Decision tree for entailment basis
The entailment basis is determined by the following procedure. Step 1, does the intervention set a term or kernel to zero. Step 2, does the outcome follow from the symbolic form alone, with no condition requiring separate confirmation. If so, the basis is E1. Step 3, otherwise, is there a specific citable result settling the outcome by entailment. If so, confirm each applicability condition with a witness. If all conditions are confirmed, the basis is E2. If any condition is unconfirmable or contested, or if any condition lacks a witness, the basis is E4. Step 4, if no citable result settles the outcome, the basis is E3. Step 5, if the analyst cannot decide, the case is logged and a second rater is required. If the sources needed to reach any of the above steps cannot be verified, the basis is not_determinable.

The definitions of E1 through E4 are in Section 7.1 and are not restated here.

7.4 Material-change test
A change is material when at least one of the following is demonstrated under a pre-specified assessment rule: disappearance, order change, class change, or a pre-registered effect-size threshold. The test is declared before the intervention. A class change is a change in the transition's classification under a stated classification scheme (universality class as defined by critical exponents; order of the transition; mean-field versus non-mean-field behavior). The scheme is recorded in material_change_test_justification. The field takes the value not_determinable when the cited numerical values on which the justification rests cannot be verified against their sources.

7.5 STAG-Discovered: prerequisites
STAG-Discovered is an extension-tier display label. Its assignment requires all of the following four prerequisites. First, effective_description_change = Yes-strong. Second, intervention_classification = Non_definitional (equivalently, entailment_basis = E3). Third, cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Yes, with a pre-registered material-change test and a recorded material-change result. Fourth, a scale-separation record satisfying the following: either scale_separation_source_kind = external_value with at least one scale value read from a source that does not take the annotated model as input, or scale_separation_source_kind = external_coefficient with both scale values computed from the model and an externally sourced coefficient entering the computation, with a declared control-parameter band recorded in the fields band_declared, band_min_ratio, band_max_ratio, band_source, and band_source_citation where applicable; and in either case a ratio greater than or equal to the declared minimum.

The four prerequisites are necessary and sufficient for the label, subject to the branch ordering in Section 7.6.

The framework does not separately define "scale transition." The four prerequisites are the sole operational criteria for the label.

Evidential anchoring is defined in Section 1.2. Prerequisite satisfaction is distinct from evidential anchoring. A case can satisfy all four prerequisites and fail anchoring on one or more of conditions (a) through (d).

The scale-separation record has the following components: the two scales compared; the quantity defining each; the source from which each value is read; scale_separation_source_kind; the unit conversion if dimensions differ, with citation; the numerical ratio; the recorded expansion parameter; band_declared; band_min_ratio; band_max_ratio; band_source; band_source_citation; analyst_dependence_acknowledged; analyst_dependence_note; dimensionless. For external_value, the record includes scale_inference_source where the value is inferred from external data via a model that does not take the annotated model as input. The field band_source_citation takes the value not_determinable when the source supporting the band cannot be verified.

The source kinds are defined as follows. external_value means the scale value is a measured quantity, or is a quantity determined by a source that does not take the annotated model as input. A sub-distinction is drawn between a direct measurement and an inference from external data via an external model; the presence of scale_inference_source marks the latter. external_coefficient means the scale values are computed from the model, but an externally sourced coefficient or exponent enters the computation, and a declared robustness band is present. model_internal means the scale value is a parameter of the model or a quantity computed from the model's own parameters alone.

The declared minimum ratio is 10, corresponding to an expansion parameter of at most 0.1. Alternative thresholds of 3, 5, or 20 are permitted with a declared justification.

A record with scale_separation_source_kind = model_internal alone is insufficient. The transition receives STAG-Unclassified with reason model_internal_scale_separation.

The four prerequisites are operational criteria, not independent evidence requirements. A single external source may satisfy multiple prerequisites if applied in different ways.

One case satisfying the four prerequisites is documented in Section 11.2. It is illustrative, not evidentially anchored.

7.6 Derived display label
The display labels are extension-tier. Derivation proceeds as follows.

Step 1. If effective_description_change = No, no transition is recorded and no label is assigned.

Step 2. If effective_description_change = Uncertain, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason uncertain.

Step 3. If effective_description_change = Yes-weak, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason weak_effective_description_basis.

Step 4. If effective_description_change = Yes-strong:

Step 4a. If cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Intra_domain:

Step 4a(i). If the material-change classification is Uncertain, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason uncertain.

Step 4a(ii). Otherwise, if intervention_classification is Definitional or Definitional(expertise-dependent), the label is STAG-Intra-Definitional.

Step 4a(iii). Otherwise, if intervention_classification = Non_definitional, the label is STAG-Intra-Non_definitional.

Step 4a(iv). Otherwise, if intervention_classification = Not_applicable, the label is STAG-Intra.

Step 4b. Otherwise, if intervention_classification is Definitional or Definitional(expertise-dependent) and the intervention is cross-domain, the label is STAG-Definitional.

Step 4c. Otherwise, if intervention_classification = Non_definitional and cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Yes and the scale-separation record satisfies the requirement in Section 7.5 with ratio at or above the minimum, the label is STAG-Discovered, reported with evidential_anchoring alongside.

Step 4d. Otherwise, if cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Yes and either the record is missing or source_kind = model_internal or the ratio is below the minimum, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason insufficient_scale_separation or model_internal_scale_separation.

Step 4e. Otherwise, if cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = No, the label is STAG-None.

Step 4f. Otherwise, if cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Not_tested, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason not_tested.

Step 4g. Otherwise, if cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Untestable, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason untestable_model or inadmissible_partition.

Step 4h. Otherwise, if cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Uncertain, the label is STAG-Unclassified with reason uncertain.

Step 4i. Otherwise, if cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = not_determinable or intervention_classification = not_determinable, the label is STAG-Definitional (for a cross-domain intervention) or STAG-Discovered (where the prerequisites are otherwise satisfied), retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside.

The label assignment does not depend on conditions (a) through (d) of evidential anchoring. A STAG-Discovered label may be assigned with shared-assumption material-change evidence, with a shared-assumption test-result relationship, with an analyst-chosen band, or with material-change evidence that is inappropriate for the anchoring criterion. Those failures are reported through evidential_anchoring and do not demote the label. The label records the annotation's structural content; the anchoring status records the strength of the evidence supporting it.

If either endpoint's regime is not_determinable, the label is retained with regime_not_determinable attached and displayed alongside. If identity_partition_sensitive = true, the label is retained with that flag attached and displayed alongside. If any core-tier field on which the label depends is not_determinable, the label is retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside.

unclassified_reason permitted values: uncertain, untestable_model, inadmissible_partition, not_tested, insufficient_scale_separation, model_internal_scale_separation, weak_effective_description_basis.

7.7 Partition pre-registration and justification
The partition must be declared before annotation and before case selection. The Intra_domain rate must be reported per partition. If a partition produces Intra_domain for more than 60 percent of transitions in a set, the partition is flagged for independent audit. For every Intra_domain label, record cross_domain_intervention_available and, if Yes, reason_if_not_performed. The 60 percent threshold is a convention; it is not derived from a statistical model.

7.8 Partition sensitivity
Partition sensitivity is required for every label above STAG-Unclassified. For STAG-Discovered, STAG-Definitional, and any STAG-Intra case, the annotation records at least one alternative admissible partition, the resulting classification, and whether the classification changed. For STAG-Discovered and STAG-Definitional with a merged node-identity decision, the identity-splitting audit of Section 5.8 is also required. For a new_node decision, the partition-sensitivity record is the required check.

7.9 What STAG-Discovered does not claim
A STAG-Discovered label does not establish that the transition is caused by the identified domains in any framework-independent sense, that the identified domains are physically fundamental, that the model is correct, that the partition is correct, that the recorded scale-separation ratio is the correct one for the system, or that the counterfactual would hold under a different model or partition. Where identity_partition_sensitive = true, the label is explicitly partition-relative. Where evidential_anchoring is not anchored, the label does not establish that an evidentially anchored case exists.

7.10 The relationship between the label and the anchoring status
The framework reports the label and the anchoring status side by side. The framework states a display convention that the anchoring status should be reported with the label in every rendering, and provides the schema (Rule 7 and Rule 26 in Artifact 2) that requires the anchoring fields to be recorded and that requires the provisional_flag to be attached to any label whose dependent fields are inconclusive. The framework cannot enforce the display convention technically. A not_determinable flag on any field is displayed alongside the label under the same convention.

The framework does not score the anchoring status into a single numeric summary. The four anchoring conditions are reported individually and are not combined.

The framework states a formal limitation on the reachability of the anchoring status. Under any rendering in which the scale-separation ratio is evaluated at a control-parameter point and the band is anchored to the phase-stability range, the ratio is bounded above by the value at the band endpoint closest to the phase boundary. The bound exists because the correlation length is finite away from the critical point and grows as the endpoint approaches the phase boundary. If the bound is below the declared minimum, the band cannot satisfy condition (b), and the anchoring status is unreachable through that band. The bound is not computed by the framework; its existence is stated here because it is the reason the reachability search in Section 13.4 is not guaranteed to succeed for profile (i) or profile (ii).

8. Domain Partitions and Graph Formalism
8.1 Decompositional and framework-level partitions
Decompositional partitions are built from a mathematically or physically distinguishable decomposition of a declared model: terms in a Lagrangian or Hamiltonian, sectors in a product-space formulation, or identifiable physical channels. Framework-level partitions distinguish whole modelling frameworks and are not admissible for cross-domain counterfactual claims; an annotation under a framework-level partition records cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence = Untestable and produces STAG-Unclassified with unclassified_reason = inadmissible_partition. A term coupling two domains is declared a boundary term.

8.2 Admissibility criteria
A decompositional partition is admissible only if the decomposition is mathematically well-defined in the declared model, each domain is assigned a distinct role and formalism, the partition is pre-registered, the partition is versioned and assigned a stable identifier, and the domain structure is documented with role tags and formalism descriptors.

8.3 Domain entry fields
domain_id; role_tags; formalism; decomposition_source; validity; status.

8.4 Partition registry
partition_id; version; domain entries; partition_kind; admissibility_status; date of creation; revision history; supersession relationships.

8.5 Graph structure and PROV-O mapping
A STAG is a directed multigraph with two kinds of nodes: state nodes and event nodes.

State nodes represent declared effective descriptions and carry the three-component tuple. Because a self-loop on a single state node would have the same prov:Entity as both prov:used and prov:generated, regime-qualified state records are represented as distinct prov:Entity instances sharing one node ID (for example N@symmetric and N@broken).

Event nodes represent transitions that cannot be represented as a single directed edge without ambiguity. An event node maps to prov:Activity. Annotation records are prov:Entity with prov:wasGeneratedBy. Integration-out and truncation relations are recorded as derived-from edges (prov:wasDerivedFrom), not as M-equivalences. Superseding annotations are new prov:Entity records with prov:wasRevisionOf.

A formal definition. A STAG is a tuple G = (V_s, V_e, E, ℓ, τ, ρ). V_s is a finite set of state nodes, each carrying a tuple (S, M, G), a node identifier, and a regime attribute. V_e is a finite set of event nodes, disjoint from V_s, used only for composite transitions. E is a finite set of directed edges, a subset of the union of V_s × V_s, V_s × V_e, and V_e × V_s. Self-loops are permitted and carry a regime_change attribute. ℓ maps V_s into S × M × G and is the node-labelling function. τ maps E into the transition-attribute record and is the edge-labelling function. ρ maps the union of E and V_e into the PROV-O mapping and is the provenance-labelling function. Well-formedness conditions: every edge has a recorded node-identity decision for its endpoints; a self-loop must carry regime_change; an event node must have at least two incoming edges from V_s or at least two outgoing edges to V_s; derived-from edges are disjoint from transition edges; supersession chains are acyclic.

The framework's formal definition does not impose constraints that would make the graph a category, a functor, or any structure richer than a labelled directed multigraph.

An abbreviated Turtle serialization of the two-layer Ising self-loop. The entity stag:N_TWO_LAYER_ISING is a prov:Entity with stag:system_scope recording the two-layer Ising model on a square lattice, stag:model recording the Hamiltonian, stag:granularity recording "coarse", stag:partition_id recording the partition identifier, and stag:partition_version recording the version. The entity stag:N_TWO_LAYER_ISING_broken is a prov:Entity with prov:specializationOf pointing to the first, stag:regime recording "broken", and stag:order_parameter recording the uniform magnetization. The activity stag:T_COUPLING_SELFLOOP is a stag:Transition and a prov:Activity, with prov:used pointing to the broken entity, prov:generated pointing to a symmetric entity, and stag:regime_change recording the transition. The full JSON Schema and SHACL schema are in Artifact 2. SHACL validation of the examples has not been run.

8.6 Edges, direction, reversibility, cycles
Edges are directed; direction does not imply irreversibility. Reversibility is an edge or event attribute: reversible, irreversible, condition-dependent, unknown. Cycles are permitted. Derived-from edges are distinct from transition edges.

8.7 Composition notation
Regime transitions: A ⇢ B. Composite formation: A + B → C. Transformations: A → B. Intra-model transitions: self-loops with a regime-change attribute. Derived-from: A ⊑ B.

8.8 Characteristic-scale conventions
A characteristic scale is a declared quantity associated with a node or event. The convention must be recorded.

9. Status Dimensions (Extension Tier)
Physical-claim status: D (derived) / E (empirical) / P (established phenomenology) / O (open or disputed). Graph-mapping status: F (formalized) / R (represented) / A (analogy). Framework-frontier status is split: underlying_science_status — established / candidate / heuristic / open; stag_implementation_status — tested / pilot-tested / untested / disputed.

10. Relationship to the Statistical Protocol
STAG does not perform statistical gap testing. The companion statistical protocol takes the annotation's transition set as input and tests for empirical gaps or discontinuities; its output is recorded as statistical_gap_result. Where the model-relative dependence classification and the statistical gap result disagree, record dependence_gap_discordance and report both.

11. Worked Examples
None of the annotations below is a validated annotation. "Worked example" means an annotated instance, not an evidentially anchored case. Full annotations are in Artifact 2.

11.1 Superconducting transition in a Fröhlich/Eliashberg model
Display label: STAG-Definitional, evidential_anchoring = not_applicable, retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside.

Partition: STAG-PARTITION-PHYSICS-FROHLICH-2024, version 1, decompositional. Domains: electronic; phononic. H_e-ph is a boundary term.

Model: Fröhlich Hamiltonian H = H_e + H_ph + H_e-ph. Eliashberg theory describes both normal and superconducting states (Eliashberg 1960; McMillan 1968; Allen and Dynes 1975). The BCS reduced Hamiltonian is obtained by integrating out phonons and truncating to the pairing channel; the channel truncation makes it a different M, recorded as a derived-from edge.

Node identity: same Hamiltonian (Fröhlich), same solution method (Eliashberg). S, M, G match. Decision: merged. Self-loop. Domain annotation: electronic.

Regime: order parameter is the s-wave pairing amplitude. T = 2 Tc (symmetric); T = 0.5 Tc (broken).

Effective-description change: Yes-strong. Criteria firing: ΔDOF (normal-state quasiparticles → Bogoliubov quasiparticles, different algebra); ΔSYM (global U(1) broken in the mean-field description); ΔCOL.

Intervention: set the electron–phonon coupling kernel to zero in the Eliashberg s-wave gap equation, retaining the electronic band structure and screened Coulomb repulsion. intervention_source_domains = {electronic, phononic}; intervention_target_domain = electronic; intervention_kind = boundary_term.

Intervention classification: not_determinable, with entailment_basis = not_determinable and cited_structural_result = not_determinable. The scope of the Eliashberg (1960) result and of the Kohn–Luttinger (1965) non-s-wave caveat could not be verified against their primary sources, so the classification is not determined. The conditions_check entry for the "no surviving channel" condition is retained but is not used to assign a class.

Cross-domain counterfactual dependence: not_determinable, since the field depends on intervention_classification, which is not_determinable. Material-change test: disappearance. Derived label: STAG-Definitional, evidential_anchoring = not_applicable, retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside, consistent with the not_determinable handling in Section 5.2.

11.2 Two-layer Ising model (illustrative, not anchored)
Display label: STAG-Discovered, evidential_anchoring = preliminary, retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside. This example illustrates the annotation mechanics. It is not evidentially anchored.

Model: two-layer Ising model on a square lattice, intralayer coupling J, interlayer coupling J_int, with Hamiltonian H = −J Σ_{⟨ij⟩∈L1} σ_i σ_j − J Σ_{⟨ij⟩∈L2} τ_i τ_j − J_int Σ_i σ_i τ_i. Parameter regime: J_int = J > 0; both layers share J.

Universality-class assumption: the claim that the coupled model at J_int = J is in the 3D Ising universality class rests on the general principle of dimensional crossover, stated as a working assumption. No specific review of dimensional crossover in layered Ising systems is cited.

Critical temperatures: T_2D ≈ 2.269 J/k_B (exact 2D Ising, Onsager 1944). The assertion that Tc(coupled) exceeds T_2D is stated as an assumption; no source is cited.

Critical exponents: ν_3D = 0.6301(4) from Ferrenberg and Landau (1991); ν_2D = 1 exact (Onsager 1944).

Partition: STAG-PARTITION-PHYSICS-TWO-LAYER-ISING-2024, version 1, decompositional. Domains: layer_1; layer_2. Interlayer term is a boundary term.

Node identity: E1 uses H with J_int = J; E2 uses H with J_int = 0. S and G match; M does not. Decision: new_node.

Regime: order parameter is the uniform magnetization m = (⟨σ⟩ + ⟨τ⟩)/2. The control-parameter point and the phase-diagram source are set to not_determinable, because the assertion that Tc(coupled) exceeds T_2D could not be verified against a source and the placement of the control-parameter point on the phase diagram is therefore not determined.

Effective-description change: Yes-strong. ΔSYM (one Z₂ symmetry coupled; two independent Z₂ symmetries decoupled); ΔDOF (one order parameter vs. two).

Intervention: set J_int = 0. intervention_source_domains = {layer_1, layer_2}; intervention_target_domain = layer_1; intervention_kind = boundary_term.

Intervention classification: Non_definitional, entailment_basis = E3. cited_numerical_result: citations Ferrenberg and Landau (1991) and Onsager (1944); classification_effect = Non_definitional; citation_scope = related_model.

Cross-domain counterfactual dependence: Yes.

Scale-separation record. Microscopic scale: lattice spacing a, dimensionless model unit. Collective scale: correlation length ξ in lattice units, evaluated at t = (T − Tc)/Tc = 0.01, with ξ/a = t^(−ν_3D) ≈ 18.2, external; ν_3D = 0.6301 from Ferrenberg and Landau (1991). Ratio: 18.2. Expansion parameter: approximately 0.055. Source kind: external_coefficient. Dimensionless: true. Control-parameter value: t = 0.01. Control-parameter source: analyst-chosen. Band declared: [0.005, 0.02]. Band source: analyst-chosen. band_source_citation is set to not_determinable, because the assertion that Tc(coupled) exceeds T_2D could not be verified against a source and the band cannot be anchored to a verified phase-stability range. band_min_ratio approximately 11.8, computed by the annotator from the band and ν_3D at t = 0.02. analyst_dependence_acknowledged = true.

Material-change test: class change in ν under the scheme "universality class as defined by critical exponents," from ν approximately 0.63 (3D Ising) at the coupled critical point to ν = 1 (2D Ising) at the decoupled critical point. material_change_test_justification is set to not_determinable, because it rests on the numerical values quoted in Allen and Dynes (1975) and McMillan (1968), which could not be verified against their sources.

Material-change result: not_determinable. The assertion that ν(coupled) is approximately 0.63 at the coupled critical point rests on the dimensional-crossover principle rather than on a verified study of the two-layer model at J_int = J, and the result is therefore not determined.

Partition sensitivity: alternative partition ...-STRONG-WEAK-2024 v1 (domains strong_layer, weak_layer); classification unchanged. Because the node-identity decision is new_node, the identity-splitting audit of Section 5.8 does not apply.

Derived display label: STAG-Discovered, evidential_anchoring = preliminary, retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside, consistent with the not_determinable handling in Section 5.2.

Anchoring assessment. The case satisfies the four prerequisites of Section 7.5. It fails anchoring on all four conditions.

Condition (a) fails. The material-change result is supported by citation to studies of the pure 3D and 2D Ising models rather than to a study of the annotated model at the annotated parameters; citation_scope = related_model.

Condition (b) fails. The band source is analyst_chosen, not external_reference, and band_source_citation is not_determinable.

Condition (c) fails. The material-change evidence rests on the same dimensional-crossover assumption that fixes the node's regime assignment at the coupled critical point. independence_of_material_change_evidence = shared_assumption.

Condition (d) fails. The material-change result is entailed by the material-change test's classification scheme applied to the model's assumed class. material_change_test_result_independence = shared_assumption.

The example is illustrative, not evidentially anchored, and it fails anchoring on grounds that are not repairable by adding a Monte Carlo study alone. A Monte Carlo study of the two-layer model at J_int = J would satisfy condition (a) and would supply an independent value of ν for condition (d). It would not supply an externally anchored band for condition (b) unless the study also established the phase-stability range over which the 3D Ising scaling regime holds. Nor would it repair condition (c), because the assumptions fixing the regime attribute are the assumptions of dimensional crossover, and these are the assumptions on which the material-change evidence would rest unless the regime assignment is established independently.

