11.6 What the examples demonstrate
The examples illustrate that the annotation records model-relative claims without overstating them, that the entailment basis separates entailment from numerical computation, that a case can satisfy the four prerequisites while failing anchoring, that the cross-domain designation is a property of the intervention and the partition, that a transition with no intervention is Not_tested rather than Intra_domain, that framework-level partitions produce STAG-Unclassified with a specific reason, and that node identity is decoupled from the domain partition and the intervention. The examples also illustrate the not_determinable handling: when a load-bearing source cannot be verified, the corresponding field is marked not_determinable and the label is retained with the flag attached and displayed alongside. None of the annotations is validated. The two-layer example of Section 11.2 is illustrative, not anchored. Whether an anchored case exists is the subject of the reachability search of Section 13.4.

12. Theory Change and Revision
Annotations are versioned. Every annotation records annotation date, theory or model version, partition identifier and version, model reference, intervention specification, effective-description criteria, evidence, annotator provenance, and revision or supersession relationships.

13. Validation Programme
13.1 Stages and phasing
Stage 0 is the ambiguity audit: eight to twelve predefined cases across at least three scientific domains, annotated by a single internal team, with the entailment basis audited with expertise stratification. The purpose is to identify ambiguities and freeze the codebook.

Stage 1a is the confirmatory core: at least twenty cases and at least three raters per case in one scientific domain, covering the five core-tier fields, the entailment basis, and the two anchoring independence fields.

Stage 1b is escalation: additional scientific domains and raters, contingent on the outcomes of Stage 1a.

Stage 2 is the extension tier: display labels and the scale-separation record, validated as a unit after the core tier has been validated.

The escalation rule: Phase 1b proceeds if and only if Phase 1a passes the governing rule of Section 13.3 for at least four of the five core-tier fields, where a threshold-sensitive field counts as passing if it passes at both 0.5 and 0.7, as failing if it fails at both, and as inconclusive if it passes at one and fails at the other.

13.2 Phase 1a design
Cases: at least twenty, drawn from named review articles by a pre-specified sampling rule. These are floors. A simulation-based power analysis precedes Phase 1a and determines the counts needed to distinguish the pre-specified agreement thresholds from chance for all five core-tier fields. The power analysis is a plan whose results will be deposited with the pre-registration; the framework does not report a power result in this paper. If the power analysis requires larger counts, the larger counts are used.

Negative controls: count set by the Clopper–Pearson one-sided 95 percent upper confidence bound for the false-positive rate, using a pre-specified error allowance of one permitted false positive and tolerance 0.20, giving twenty-two controls. Controls are stratified into obvious_equivalence (basis changes, gauge fixings, field redefinitions, equivalent mean-field reorganizations) and non_obvious_equivalence (dualities such as Kramers–Wannier, exact bosonization identities, non-trivial exact mappings). Because ΔDOF is defined to exclude exact reparametrizations, testing it on obvious equivalences is close to tautological. The false-positive rate is reported separately for the two strata, and the tolerance of 0.20 must hold for the non_obvious_equivalence stratum for the criterion to be regarded as passing. Each control is independently confirmed to be an exact reparametrization by at least two domain experts. The framework does not prove that the control set is exhaustive over the space of exact reparametrizations; the reported false-positive rate is a lower bound.

Raters: domain experts with relevant expertise per case, working independently with the frozen codebook and one training example from the audit.

Fields rated: the five core-tier fields, the entailment basis, and the two anchoring independence fields.

Rating procedure for independence_of_material_change_evidence. The rater lists the assumptions used to fix node identity, the regime attribute, and the effective-description-change classification. The rater lists the assumptions underlying the material-change evidence. The rater classifies as independent if the two lists are disjoint, as shared_assumption if they intersect, and as not_assessed if the rater cannot determine either list.

Rating procedure for material_change_test_result_independence. The rater writes down the classification scheme of the material-change test and the assumptions used to compute the result. The rater classifies as independent if the result does not follow from applying the scheme to those assumptions alone, without additional computation; as shared_assumption if it does; and as not_assessed if the rater cannot decide.

Node-set agreement: measured on the pairwise merge decision over the plausible-merge set, defined in Section 5.7 as pairs of candidate descriptions differing in at least one of S, M, G. A sensitivity analysis uses pairs differing in exactly one component. Agreement is computed with Krippendorff's alpha on the pairwise decisions, with the case identifier used as a cluster for the bootstrap confidence interval.

13.3 Agreement statistics and the governing rule
Per field: raw agreement, Krippendorff's alpha, Gwet's AC1, prevalence per category.

Governing rule: the lower bound of the 95 percent confidence interval on alpha must be at least 0.6, and the point estimate of AC1 must be at least 0.6. Both pass leads to pass. Alpha passes but AC1 fails leads to inconclusive (prevalence-sensitive). Alpha fails but AC1 passes leads to inconclusive (prevalence-sensitive). Both fail leads to fail.

The threshold of 0.6 is a convention chosen for exploratory annotation intended for reuse by a second analyst, not for confirmatory measurement of an individual case. The pass/fail status of a field is threshold-dependent, and the falsification conditions that invoke the governing rule inherit that dependence.

Threshold sensitivity: a sensitivity analysis of the agreement outcome at thresholds of 0.5 and 0.7 is reported. If a field's outcome differs between the two thresholds, the field is reported as threshold-sensitive (threshold_sensitive_flag = true), with both outcomes reported. A threshold-sensitive field counts as passing for the escalation rule of Section 13.1 if it passes at both thresholds, as failing if it fails at both, and as inconclusive if it passes at one and fails at the other.

Consequence of inconclusive: the field is usable for annotation but is flagged. The field's agreement_outcome is inconclusive and provisional_flag is true. Any display label depending on that field is reported with provisional_flag attached. After the revision cycle, a field that remains inconclusive narrows the framework's claims on any dependent label.

All-inconclusive scenario: if, after the revision cycle, three or more core-tier fields remain inconclusive and none is fail, the framework is reported as not validated but not falsified. The contribution claim narrows to the specification, and no claim is made about reliability of the display labels.

Confidence intervals: case-level cluster bootstrap. The regime attribute's not_applicable and not_determinable values are retained and the agreement statistic is computed on the full value set; a separate analysis excluding these two values is reported as a sensitivity check. Reliability is not validity.

Revision policy: a single revision cycle after Phase 1a. Poor agreement means fail under the governing rule.

13.4 Validity evidence and the reachability hypothesis
Route 1, synthetic ground-truth comparison. The 2D Ising intra-domain case validates the entailment basis and the intra-domain precedence rule; evidential_anchoring = not_applicable. The two-layer Ising case is illustrative of the annotation mechanics and not evidentially anchored; evidential_anchoring = preliminary. The coupled nonlinear oscillators case (to be described in the supplementary deposit) is preliminary for the E3 basis. Additional toy models to be added include a two-band gap equation with a non-separable kernel, a mean-field model with competing order parameters, and a lattice model with a removable interlayer coupling; evidential_anchoring = candidate.

Route 2, independent computational replication. For a published claim that removing a term changes an outcome, an independent analyst or script re-solves the model using the published specification and parameters. Route 2 validates the source's claim under the annotation's stated intervention, not the annotation's dependence classification.

Route 3, scientific-value test, in Section 13.6.

Reachability hypothesis: at least one published physical system, or at least K synthetic constructions, is evidentially anchored, with all four anchoring conditions satisfied. The three target profiles are stated in Section 1.2. Profile (i) is preferred. Profile (ii) is acceptable if the inference source is external and the inference is declared. Profile (iii) is acceptable only with a declared robustness band. The pre-registered value of K is deposited with the validation protocol. The target outcome is at least one case with evidential_anchoring = anchored.

A limitation of the target profiles. Profile (i) requires an externally anchored band and a ratio above the minimum throughout the band. Section 7.10 states the bound on the ratio at the band endpoint closest to the phase boundary. If the bound is below the declared minimum, profile (i) is unreachable for that band. Profile (ii) has the same problem, and additionally may be empty because many real correlation lengths are inferred via models that are themselves the annotated model. Profile (iii) is a synthetic construction with an externally specified robustness band; this profile is reachable by construction if the constructor chooses the band appropriately, but the framework does not claim that such a synthetic construction is scientifically interesting.

Falsification. If the pre-registered search exhausts its candidate pool and finds no published physical system and fewer than K synthetic constructions satisfying the anchoring definition, the reachability hypothesis is falsified for the domains searched. The consequence is that the contribution narrows to the annotation architecture with the anchoring status retained as a defined but uninstantiated field. Feature (c) of the contribution claim remains specified. The paper should be reframed as a limitative result about the difficulty of constructing an evidence-independent scale-transition discovery criterion. Partial evidence, such as a search finding fewer candidates than the pool size or failures concentrated in one reason category, is reported as inconclusive.

Validity evidence is reported separately from inter-rater agreement.

13.5 Comparison with established descriptions
Reference standard: domain experts classify each candidate blinded to STAG. For each pair, the expert decides whether the two descriptions use the same Hamiltonian or Lagrangian under the declared M-equivalence rule, and whether an effective-description change is present. Fields compared: effective-description change and the M component of node identity. Metric: chance-corrected agreement per field. The reference standard is not independent of the framework in the sense that the experts must apply the framework's M-equivalence rule.

13.6 Independent empirical application and scientific-value test
Application: a real scientific case selected independently of the framework's development.

Primary endpoint: whether an independent analyst can use the STAG annotation to identify a transition whose cross-domain dependence status was not apparent from the free-text description, or to predict the outcome of an intervention not described in the source. Measured as the rate at which the analyst correctly identifies dependence or predicts the intervention outcome.

Control condition: information-matched. A domain expert, working from the same source text and citations, writes a structured summary of equivalent length and content to the STAG annotation, using the domain's own vocabulary and no STAG structure, no display label, and no codebook.

Treatment condition: the same source text and citations, plus the STAG display label, plus the annotation JSON fields listed in the reporting checklist, excluding agreement_outcome, provisional_flag, and confidence.

Matching adequacy. The matched control is assessed by a pre-registered rubric applied by two independent raters. The rubric names the dimensions on which matching is judged: the number of retrievable facts, the number of causal or dependence relations stated, the number of evidential qualifiers stated, and the length in tokens. The matching is accepted if both raters judge it adequate. If the raters disagree, the case is excluded from the scientific-value analysis and the exclusion is reported.

Cost endpoints, operationally defined. Annotation time: wall-clock time from the start of the annotation to its deposit. Expert time: sum of expert-hours spent. Retrieval time: wall-clock time for an independent analyst to locate the transition. Error count: number of correction events logged in the annotation's revision history.

All other endpoints are declared secondary and corrected for multiple comparisons.

Success criterion: STAG adds scientific value only if it improves the primary endpoint relative to the information-matched control, with a pre-specified effect size and confidence interval, and if the cost is disclosed and judged acceptable. The success criterion is not evaluated in this paper.

13.7 Falsification conditions
The following conditions are the falsification conditions for the framework as a whole and for its individual fields. Each condition is stated with the operational test that is applied. A condition is evaluated on the frozen codebook and the pre-registered analysis plan; a condition is not evaluated post hoc.

Condition F1. Node-identity agreement. STAG fails on this field if node-identity agreement fails under the governing rule of Section 13.3 after the single revision cycle permitted by Section 13.3. Operational test: compute Krippendorff's alpha and Gwet's AC1 on the pairwise merge decisions for the plausible-merge set defined in Section 5.7, apply the four-way governing table of Section 13.3, and check whether the outcome is fail after the revision cycle. A threshold-sensitive outcome is reported with both outcomes at 0.5 and 0.7; if the two thresholds disagree, the field is reported as threshold-sensitive and the condition is not triggered unless both thresholds produce fail.

Condition F2. Regime-attribute agreement. STAG fails on this field if regime-attribute agreement fails under the governing rule of Section 13.3 after the revision cycle. Operational test: compute the same statistics on the regime attribute with not_applicable and not_determinable retained in the value set, and with the excluding-them sensitivity analysis reported alongside; check whether the outcome is fail under the same threshold-sensitivity rule as F1.

Condition F3. Effective-description change agreement. STAG fails on this field if effective-description change agreement fails under the governing rule of Section 13.3 after the revision cycle. Operational test: compute the same statistics on the effective-description change field with the full value set (Yes-strong, Yes-weak, No, Uncertain), and check whether the outcome is fail under the same threshold-sensitivity rule as F1.

Condition F4. Scientific-value endpoint. STAG fails if, on the confirmatory set, STAG does not improve the pre-specified scientific-value primary endpoint of Section 13.6 relative to the information-matched control. Operational test: compute the rate at which the analyst correctly identifies dependence or predicts the intervention outcome in each condition; the condition is triggered if the treatment rate does not exceed the control rate at the pre-registered effect size and confidence interval. The pre-registered effect size and confidence interval are deposited with the validation protocol.

Condition F5. Theory revision. STAG fails if the framework cannot represent theory revision without losing provenance. Operational test: a case is selected in which a published model has been superseded by a later model; the annotator is required to record the revision using the supersedes and superseded_by fields of Section 12 and to preserve the annotation history. The condition is triggered if the revision cannot be recorded without overwriting an earlier annotation record.

Condition F6. Display-label distinguishability. STAG fails if the summary display labels of Section 1.3 cannot be distinguished reliably under the frozen codebook. Operational test: compute the governing statistics on the pairwise distinction between each pair of display labels in the confirmatory set; the condition is triggered if the outcome is fail for the label-as-a-unit analysis of Phase 2, after the revision cycle.

Condition F7. Threshold-provenance summary-label reproducibility. STAG fails on the threshold-provenance extension field if threshold-provenance summary labels cannot be reproduced at the pre-specified level. Operational test: compute the governing statistics on the threshold-provenance summary labels (Class 1, Class 2, Class 3, Mixed, Unknown) in the extension tier; the condition is triggered if the outcome is fail after the revision cycle.

Condition F8. Core-tier field redundancy. A core-tier field is redundant if both of the following hold: (i) the field is predictable from the remaining core-tier fields with a held-out accuracy of at least 0.80, and (ii) dropping the field changes no derived label for at least 20 percent of cases. Both constants are pre-registered values. Operational test: fit a predictive model from the remaining core-tier fields to the field of interest on a training split of the confirmatory set, compute held-out accuracy on a held-out split, and compare against 0.80. Separately, re-derive the display labels for the confirmatory set with the field of interest removed and record the fraction of cases for which no label changes. The condition is triggered if the held-out accuracy is at least 0.80 and the no-change fraction is at least 0.20. A field flagged as redundant is a candidate for removal; the condition is applied field by field, not to the framework as a whole.

Condition F9. Legitimate-case accommodation. STAG fails if the framework cannot accommodate scientifically legitimate cases without forcing them into inappropriate categories. Operational test: the ambiguity audit of Stage 0 (Section 13.1) and the confirmatory set of Stage 1a are searched for cases in which the annotator has recorded that the framework's categories are a poor fit. A case is counted as an accommodation failure if two independent raters, working from the annotator's written rationale, agree that the framework's categories are an inappropriate fit and that a scientifically legitimate characterization of the case is excluded. The condition is triggered if the count of agreed accommodation failures is at least the pre-registered tolerance, which is 2 cases out of the confirmatory set of 20 or more. Both the count and the case rationales are reported.

Condition F10. ΔDOF false-positive rate. STAG fails on the ΔDOF criterion if the upper 95 percent confidence bound on the ΔDOF false-positive rate over the non_obvious_equivalence control stratum exceeds 0.20. Operational test: for each control in the non_obvious_equivalence stratum, record whether ΔDOF fires; compute the Clopper–Pearson one-sided 95 percent upper bound on the false-positive rate; the condition is triggered if the upper bound exceeds 0.20. The bound is also reported for the obvious_equivalence stratum, but the condition applies to the non_obvious_equivalence stratum only.

Condition F11. Intra-domain rate and partition adequacy. STAG fails on a given partition if the Intra_domain rate for that partition, computed over the confirmatory set, exceeds 0.60, and either the partition justification is judged inadequate or the independent audit finds the partition inappropriate. Operational test: compute the Intra_domain rate over the confirmatory set for each partition; if the rate exceeds 0.60, trigger the audit procedure of Section 7.7 and record the auditor's judgment of the partition justification. The condition is triggered if the rate exceeds 0.60 and either the audit finds the justification inadequate or the independent audit finds the partition inappropriate.

Condition F12. Synthetic ground-truth comparison. STAG fails if, on the synthetic ground-truth comparison, the rater's label disagrees with the known ground truth above the pre-registered tolerance, not attributable to the entailment basis or the scale-separation record. Operational test: the rater is given the synthetic cases with known ground-truth labels and is required to assign a display label; disagreement is counted per case; the condition is triggered if the disagreement rate exceeds the pre-registered tolerance and, on review of the disagreement cases, the disagreement is not attributable to the entailment basis or the scale-separation record. The pre-registered tolerance is 0.20 of the synthetic cases. The attribution review is performed by two independent raters using a pre-registered attribution rubric.

Condition F13. Entailment-basis agreement. STAG fails on the entailment basis if the field fails under the governing rule of Section 13.3 after the revision cycle. Operational test: compute the same statistics on entailment_basis with the value set E1, E2, E3, E4, and not_determinable; check whether the outcome is fail under the same threshold-sensitivity rule as F1. If the condition is triggered, STAG-Definitional and the STAG-Discovered label's dependence on the entailment classification are reported as provisional.

Condition F14. Reachability hypothesis. STAG's reachability hypothesis is falsified if the pre-registered search of Section 13.4 exhausts its candidate pool and finds no published physical system and fewer than K synthetic constructions satisfying the anchoring definition of Section 1.2, for all target domains searched. Operational test: execute the pre-registered search protocol deposited in Supplementary Material D; record each candidate's profile type and the reason for any failure; the condition is triggered if the exhaustion criterion is met for all target domains. The pre-registered value of K is deposited with the validation protocol. The consequence of the condition is stated in Section 13.4.

Fields reported as inconclusive. Fields whose governing-rule outcome is inconclusive are not triggers of any falsification condition. They are provisionally acceptable but not validated, and any dependent label is reported with provisional_flag attached. After the revision cycle, a field that remains inconclusive narrows the framework's claims on any dependent label, as stated in Section 13.3.

