14.2 Implementation sketch
A practical STAG database consists of five linked registries: node, event, partition, model, revision. Node entries carry the three-tuple and the M-matching rule version. Domain annotations are stored in a separate domain-annotation table linked to node and partition. Event entries carry transition attributes; self-loops carry the regime-change attribute. Edge and event entries carry the node-identity decision, the regime attribute, intervention_kind, the domain_mismatch flag, and the scale-separation record where STAG-Discovered is claimed.

14.3 Limitations
Grouped by theme. Each item is stated once.

Identity limitations.

Node identity is tuple-relative but not partition-relative or intervention-relative. Node-set agreement is not tested on regime- or domain-differing pairs. Diagnostic: the identity-splitting audit of Section 5.8, which applies only to merged nodes and does not repair the under-splitting.

Only M is externally anchored. S and G are settled by the pre-registered matching rule.

The three-component rule has not been stress-tested on multi-domain Hamiltonians under multiple alternative partitions.

Evidence limitations.

M-equivalence is restricted to exact reparametrizations. Integrating out a field gives the same M only for the exact effective action.

The framework presupposes established phase diagrams. Absent one, the regime attribute is not_determinable and the flag attaches.

Cross-domain counterfactual dependence has no independent expert reference. It is validated by internal agreement, synthetic ground truth, and independent computational replication.

The entailment classification is expertise-dependent and is not given a proof-theoretic semantics. The definitional case of Section 11.1 is marked not_determinable because the scope of the Eliashberg (1960) result and of the Kohn–Luttinger (1965) non-s-wave caveat could not be verified against their primary sources.

Condition (c) of evidential anchoring cannot be repaired by adding an independent measurement of the material-change result, because the condition concerns the relation between the material-change evidence and the assumptions fixing the regime, identity, and description-change classification. Section 11.2 is a case in point.

Condition (d) of evidential anchoring is rated by an analyst who must decide whether the result is entailed by the test's scheme applied to the test's assumptions. The rating is expertise-dependent.

The negative-control set is not proved exhaustive over the space of exact reparametrizations. The reported false-positive rate is a lower bound.

Verification limitations.

Verification status of empirical inputs. Eight load-bearing numerical or attributive claims listed in Section 14.4 were checked against their sources. The 3D Ising critical exponent ν_3D = 0.6301(4) attributed to Ferrenberg and Landau (1991), the 2D Ising critical temperature T_2D ≈ 2.269 J/k_B attributed to Onsager (1944), the exactness of ν = 1 in the 2D Ising model, the attribution of the spontaneous magnetization result to Yang (1952), and the attribution of the duality transformation to Kramers and Wannier (1941) were verified. Three items could not be verified against their primary sources: the scope of the Eliashberg (1960) result and the Kohn–Luttinger (1965) non-s-wave caveat; the numerical values quoted in Allen and Dynes (1975) and McMillan (1968); and the assertion that Tc(coupled) exceeds T_2D for the two-layer Ising model. The corresponding annotation fields are marked not_determinable rather than populated from memory, in accordance with the rule stated in Section 14.4. The affected labels are retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside.

Validation limitations.

STAG-Discovered has one illustrative example (Section 11.2), which is not evidentially anchored. Whether it can be converted into an anchored case is an open question. The framework gives reasons to expect that the conversion is blocked for conditions (b) and (c), and not only for condition (a).

Reachability of an anchored STAG-Discovered case is unestablished. The reachability search is pre-registered. The preferred target profile (i) may be empty in practice. The acceptable alternative profile (ii) may also be empty, because the inference model is often the annotated model itself.

The scale-separation requirement for evidential anchoring requires an externally anchored band. Where the band is analyst-chosen, anchoring fails on condition (b). The analyst_dependence_acknowledged flag records the analyst's assessment and is not independently verified.

The governing agreement rule reports inconclusive as a distinct outcome, and the outcome is threshold-dependent. A threshold-sensitive field is reported with both outcomes and counted by the rule in Section 13.3.

The all-inconclusive scenario is governed by an explicit rule. If three or more core-tier fields remain inconclusive and none fails, the framework is reported as not validated but not falsified.

Inter-rater reliability has not been demonstrated. Reliability is not validity.

Phase 1a is a target design. If it cannot be run at the specified scale, the framework narrows to a smaller set of domains or is reported as exploratory.

The scoping review has not been executed. The feature matrix is a draft.

The scientific-value test's information-matched control is assessed by a rubric whose dimensions are stated but not validated.

Formalism limitations.

The framework's graph formalism is a labelled directed multigraph and nothing richer. A reader who requires categorical structure will not find it.

The four anchoring conditions are not of the same logical type and are not combined into a single score. A reader who requires a formally unified notion of evidential strength will not find one.

Framework-level partitions do not support counterfactual claims.

The EXPERT criterion introduces judgement and is analysed separately.

SHACL validation of the examples has not been run.

Convention limitations.

The 0.6 agreement threshold and the 0.20 false-positive tolerance are conventions. They are reported as conventions and the sensitivity analysis at 0.5 and 0.7 is reported alongside.

The 60 percent intra-domain threshold is a convention and is reported as a convention.

The declared minimum ratio of 10 is a convention and is reported as a convention with alternatives stated.

14.4 Verification status of empirical inputs
The following load-bearing numerical or attributive claims were checked against their sources. The 3D Ising critical exponent ν_3D = 0.6301(4) attributed to Ferrenberg and Landau (1991) was verified. The 2D Ising critical temperature T_2D ≈ 2.269 J/k_B attributed to Onsager (1944) was verified. The exactness of ν = 1 in the 2D Ising model was verified. The attribution of the two-dimensional spontaneous magnetization result to Yang (1952) was verified. The attribution of the duality transformation to Kramers and Wannier (1941) was verified.

The following items were not verifiable against their primary sources and the corresponding annotation fields are marked not_determinable rather than populated from memory. The scope of the Eliashberg (1960) result and of the Kohn–Luttinger (1965) non-s-wave caveat could not be verified; the fields intervention_classification, entailment_basis, and cited_structural_result for the annotation of Section 11.1 are marked not_determinable. The numerical values quoted in Allen and Dynes (1975) and McMillan (1968) could not be verified; the fields cited_numerical_result and material_change_test_justification for any annotation citing these values are marked not_determinable. The assertion that Tc(coupled) exceeds T_2D for the two-layer Ising model could not be verified; the regime attribute's control-parameter point and phase-diagram source, the material_change_result, and the band_source_citation field of the scale-separation record for the annotation of Section 11.2 are marked not_determinable.

15. Conclusion
STAG is proposed as a provenance-aware annotation standard for claims about scale-dependent scientific organization, together with a pre-registered scoping review. It is a specification whose usefulness is an empirical question.

Principal safeguards. Classify before interpreting. Define units before constructing the graph, with node identity drawn from (S, M, G) alone. Match M on Hamiltonian or Lagrangian under an exact-reparametrization rule with a recorded witness. Record regime as an attribute with a documented order-parameter source and a not_determinable fallback. Use only admissible decompositional partitions for counterfactual claims. Separate structural entailment (E1) and entailment-with-confirmed-conditions (E2) from new computation (E3) and contested classification (E4), with per-condition witnesses. Require Yes-strong effective-description change for STAG-Discovered. Require a scale-separation record with an externally read scale value or an externally sourced coefficient with a declared control-parameter band, and a ratio exceeding the minimum across an externally anchored band for evidential anchoring. Require material-change evidence independent of the assumptions fixing identity, regime, and description change. Require the material-change test and result not to share an assumption entailing the result. Include negative controls sized by a pre-specified error allowance, stratified into obvious and non-obvious equivalences. Use a governing agreement rule that reports inconclusive as a distinct outcome and propagates the flag, with an explicit rule for the all-inconclusive case and an explicit acknowledgment that the outcome is threshold-dependent. Validate the cross-domain dependence field by synthetic ground truth and independent computational replication. Version annotations. Validate the core tier before the extension tier. Treat reliability as necessary but not sufficient for validity. Mark any field whose load-bearing source cannot be verified as not_determinable, and retain the label with the flag attached and displayed alongside.

The worked examples cover STAG-Definitional, STAG-Discovered, STAG-None, STAG-Intra-Definitional, and STAG-Unclassified. The two-layer Ising case of Section 11.2 is illustrative, not anchored. It fails all four anchoring conditions, and the failures on conditions (b) and (c) are not repairable by adding a Monte Carlo study of the model. Whether an anchored STAG-Discovered case exists is the subject of the pre-registered reachability search of Section 13.4. The framework does not claim that the anchoring status is currently instantiated.

Three load-bearing empirical inputs could not be verified against their primary sources, and the corresponding annotation fields are marked not_determinable throughout the framework: the scope of the Eliashberg (1960) result and of the Kohn–Luttinger (1965) non-s-wave caveat, the numerical values quoted in Allen and Dynes (1975) and McMillan (1968), and the assertion that Tc(coupled) exceeds T_2D for the two-layer Ising model. The disclosure is stated in Section 14.3 and Section 14.4, and the affected labels are retained with the not_determinable flag attached and displayed alongside.

The proposed contribution is the integration of effective field theory, renormalization-group methods, multiscale modelling, ontology, and formal causal modelling into a single annotation architecture, subject to confirmation by the scoped search. Whether that contribution is useful is an empirical question. Whether the anchoring status is reachable is a second empirical question, and the framework's scope depends on it.

